---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.5 — Loop Analysis, Fencepost Traps &amp; The LoopTracer Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.9 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> On the AP CS A exam, nearly a
    quarter of Multiple Choice questions ask you to determine how many times a loop executes, spot
    an off-by-one ("fencepost") error, or trace a loop whose variable both gains and loses value
    within the cycle. Today you build <code>LoopTracer.java</code> and confront the nastiest
    loop-analysis traps on the exam.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Spec-First Workflow (Download Both Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/LoopTracer.java" download>LoopTracer.java</a> — starter stub where you
            will assemble all four methods.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/LoopTracerCheck.java" download>LoopTracerCheck.java</a> — final
            acceptance verification suite (17 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong> On AP MC and FRQ problems, you must trace loop counts
        and edge cases by hand with zero test harness. For each part below, trace the tricky edge
        cases on paper first, draft the code pieces in the prompts, combine them in
        <code>LoopTracer.java</code>, and test your own boundary values <em>before</em> running
        <code>LoopTracerCheck</code> at the end.
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac LoopTracer.java LoopTracerCheck.java
java LoopTracerCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · Guard Priority &amp; Fenceposts</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~40 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countWhileIterations(int start, int limit, int step)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Return the exact number of times the body of this loop executes:
    </p>
    <pre><code>int val = start;
while (val &lt; limit) {
    // body
    val += step;
}</code></pre>
    <ul>
        <li>If <code>start &gt;= limit</code>, the condition is immediately <code>false</code> on
            the first check, so the loop runs <code>0</code> times (even if <code>step &lt;= 0</code>!).</li>
        <li>If <code>start &lt; limit</code> and <code>step &lt;= 0</code>, the loop would run
            forever; return <code>-1</code> without hanging.</li>
        <li>Otherwise, simulate or compute the exact number of iterations.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>(0, 10, 3)</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> (<code>val</code> visits <code>0, 3, 6, 9</code>).</li>
                <li><code>(2, 10, 4)</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (<code>val</code> visits <code>2, 6</code>; stops at <code>10</code> since <code>10 &lt; 10</code> is <code>false</code>).</li>
                <li><code>(12, 10, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (loop test is <code>false</code> immediately!).</li>
                <li><code>(0, 10, 0)</code> or <code>(0, 10, -2)</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code> (infinite loop).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>countWhileIterations</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Guard Priority):</strong> Suppose a student checks
                <code>if (step &lt;= 0) return -1;</code> <em>before</em> checking
                <code>if (start &gt;= limit) return 0;</code>. Which test input breaks that order,
                and what are the two guards in the right order?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Simulation Loop &amp; Return):</strong> Write the loop and counter
                that counts exact iterations once you know <code>start &lt; limit</code> and
                <code>step &gt; 0</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B inside <code>countWhileIterations</code> in
            <code>LoopTracer.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>buildRunedFence(String rune, int pillars)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Build a fence banner containing <code>pillars</code> copies of <code>"[" + rune + "]"</code>
        connected by <code>"---"</code> dividers:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>buildRunedFence("*", 3)</code> &rarr; <code>"[*]---[*]---[*]"</code> (3 pillars,
            <strong>2</strong> dividers, no trailing <code>---</code>).</li>
        <li><code>buildRunedFence("SOL", 1)</code> &rarr; <code>"[SOL]"</code> (1 pillar,
            <strong>0</strong> dividers).</li>
        <li>If <code>pillars &lt;= 0</code>, return <code>""</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: The Fencepost Problem</h3>
        <p>
            To build a 3-pillar fence, there are <code>3</code> pillars (<code>[*]</code>) but only
            <code>2</code> spans of wire (<code>---</code>) between them.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>If a loop simply runs <code>pillars</code> times and does
                <code>result += "[" + rune + "]---";</code> every iteration, what does
                <code>buildRunedFence("*", 3)</code> return, and why does it fail?</li>
            <li>Explain the "Fencepost Pattern": how do you place the first post outside the loop
                (or check the loop index) so there are always <code>pillars - 1</code> dividers for
                <code>pillars</code> posts?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>buildRunedFence</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Non-Positive Guard &amp; First Post):</strong> Write the guard for
                <code>pillars &lt;= 0</code> and the initial <code>result</code> holding the very
                first pillar?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Divider + Post Loop):</strong> Write the <code>for</code> loop
                that appends the remaining <code>pillars - 1</code> spans of
                <code>"---[" + rune + "]"</code> and returns <code>result</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble <code>buildRunedFence</code> in <code>LoopTracer.java</code> and trace
            <code>("*", 0)</code>, <code>("SOL", 1)</code>, and <code>("*", 3)</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 3 &amp; 4 · Spec, Edge Cases &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>firstTurnReachingMana(int startMana, int gainPerTurn, int drainEveryThirdTurn, int targetMana)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        A mage starts with <code>startMana</code>. If <code>startMana &gt;= targetMana</code>
        before Turn 1 even begins, return <code>0</code>. Otherwise, during each turn
        <code>turn = 1, 2, 3, ...</code>:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>The mage gains <code>gainPerTurn</code> mana.</li>
        <li>If <code>turn</code> is a multiple of <code>3</code> (<code>turn % 3 == 0</code>), a
            curse drains <code>drainEveryThirdTurn</code> mana during that same turn.</li>
        <li>At the end of the turn (after any Turn-3 drain), check if mana is now
            <code>&gt;= targetMana</code>. If so, return <code>turn</code>!</li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li>If the mage will <strong>never</strong> reach <code>targetMana</code>, return
            <code>-1</code> without entering an infinite loop.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>(20, 5, 2, 15)</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (already at or above target before Turn 1).</li>
                <li><code>(0, 5, 4, 15)</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> (T1: <code>5</code>, T2: <code>10</code>, T3: <code>10 + 5 - 4 = 11</code>, T4: <code>11 + 5 = 16 &gt;= 15</code>).</li>
                <li><code>(0, 2, 6, 10)</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code> (every 3 turns nets <code>0</code>, never reaches <code>10</code>).</li>
                <li><code>(6, 2, 10, 10)</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (reaches <code>10</code> on Turn 2 <em>before</em> the Turn-3 drain fires!).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: The Peak-Before-Drain Trap &amp; Turn-3 Ordering</h3>
        <p>
            Over every 3-turn cycle, the mage's net mana change is
            <code>3 * gainPerTurn - drainEveryThirdTurn</code>. Suppose a programmer writes this
            infinite-loop check at the very top of the method:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive guard — why does this fail when the mage peaks early?
if (3 * gainPerTurn - drainEveryThirdTurn &lt;= 0) {
    return -1;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace <code>firstTurnReachingMana(6, 2, 10, 10)</code>: even though the 3-turn net
                change is negative (<code>6 - 10 = -4</code>), what is the mage's mana at the end
                of Turn 1 and Turn 2? Why does the mage win on Turn 2 <em>before</em> the Turn-3
                drain ever fires?</li>
            <li>Trace <code>firstTurnReachingMana(0, 5, 4, 15)</code> on Turn 3: why would checking
                <code>if (mana &gt;= targetMana)</code> <em>before</em> subtracting
                <code>drainEveryThirdTurn</code> wrongly return <code>3</code> instead of
                <code>4</code>?</li>
            <li>Why is it safe to simulate Turn 1, Turn 2, and Turn 3 first, and only return
                <code>-1</code> if the mage hasn't reached <code>targetMana</code> by the end of
                Turn 3 AND their mana at the end of Turn 3 is <code>&lt;= startMana</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>firstTurnReachingMana</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Already-At-Target Guard &amp; Setup):</strong> Write the
                <code>startMana &gt;= targetMana</code> guard and initialize <code>mana</code> and
                <code>turn</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Turn Loop, Intra-Turn Order &amp; Cycle Guard):</strong> Inside a
                <code>while (true)</code> loop, write the exact sequence for one turn: increment
                <code>turn</code>, add <code>gainPerTurn</code>, conditionally apply
                <code>drainEveryThirdTurn</code> on multiples of 3, check for victory
                (<code>mana &gt;= targetMana</code>), and check the Turn-3 stall guard
                (<code>turn == 3 &amp;&amp; mana &lt;= startMana</code>)?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B inside <code>firstTurnReachingMana</code> in
            <code>LoopTracer.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>countTriangularSteps(int n)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Return the total number of times <code>steps++</code> executes in this nested loop (or
        <code>0</code> if <code>n &lt;= 0</code>):
    </p>
    <pre><code>int steps = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
    for (int j = i; j &lt;= n; j += 2) {
        steps++;
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Trace &amp; Build: Hand-Trace Before Coding</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Hand-trace the exact <code>(i, j)</code> pairs and total <code>steps</code> for
                <code>n = 4</code> and <code>n = 5</code> (remember <code>j</code> jumps by
                <code>2</code>!)?</li>
            <li>How does the outer loop condition <code>i &lt;= n</code> handle
                <code>n &lt;= 0</code> automatically without an extra <code>if</code> guard?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Implement <code>countTriangularSteps</code> in <code>LoopTracer.java</code>. Test all
            four methods in a scratch <code>main</code> against your hand-traced values, and
            <strong>only then</strong> run <code>java LoopTracerCheck</code> as final verification.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods in <code>LoopTracer.java</code> were built from your pieces and verified against your own traces first</li>
            <li><code>java LoopTracerCheck</code> reports <strong><code>17 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection and code-piece boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a> — trace tables, off-by-one, iteration counts</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.5" title="4.5 Loop Analysis, Fencepost Traps &amp; LoopTracer Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
